Which congruence test proves that this pair of triangles is congruent?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.85]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tickmark2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (V) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (2.0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (1.606,2.294);
\draw[tickmark] (V) -- (A);
\draw[tickmark2] (V) -- (B);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.5cm] {angle=A--V--B};
\coordinate (V2) at (7.0,0);
\coordinate (A2) at (5.0,0);
\coordinate (B2) at (5.394,2.294);
\draw[tickmark] (V2) -- (A2);
\draw[tickmark2] (V2) -- (B2);
\draw (A2) -- (B2);
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.5cm] {angle=B2--V2--A2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Which congruence test proves that this pair of triangles is congruent?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.85]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tickmark2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (Q) at (0,0);
\coordinate (R) at (2.9,0);
\coordinate (P) at (0,2.0);
\draw[tickmark2] (Q) -- (R);
\draw (Q) -- (P);
\draw[tickmark] (P) -- (R);
\coordinate (qa) at ($(Q)!0.3cm!(R)$);
\coordinate (qb) at ($(Q)!0.3cm!(P)$);
\draw (qa) -- ($(qa)+(qb)-(Q)$) -- (qb);
\coordinate (Q2) at (7.6,0);
\coordinate (R2) at (4.7,0);
\coordinate (P2) at (7.6,2.0);
\draw[tickmark2] (Q2) -- (R2);
\draw (Q2) -- (P2);
\draw[tickmark] (P2) -- (R2);
\coordinate (qc) at ($(Q2)!0.3cm!(R2)$);
\coordinate (qd) at ($(Q2)!0.3cm!(P2)$);
\draw (qc) -- ($(qc)+(qd)-(Q2)$) -- (qd);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Which congruence test proves that these two triangles are congruent?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=1.0]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4.05,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.75,1.414);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$9\text{ cm}$};
\draw (A) -- (C) node[midway,above left,font=\small] {$5\text{ cm}$};
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway,above right,font=\small] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\coordinate (A2) at (9.3,0);
\coordinate (B2) at (5.25,0);
\coordinate (C2) at (7.55,1.414);
\draw (A2) -- (B2) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$9\text{ cm}$};
\draw (A2) -- (C2) node[midway,above right,font=\small] {$5\text{ cm}$};
\draw (B2) -- (C2) node[midway,above left,font=\small] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The three angles of one triangle equal the three angles of the other.
What can be concluded?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.4,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.674,1.995);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
\pic[draw,"$50^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.5cm,angle eccentricity=2.0] {angle=B--A--C};
\pic[draw,"$70^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.42cm,angle eccentricity=2.2] {angle=C--B--A};
\coordinate (A2) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (B2) at (8.04,0);
\coordinate (C2) at (6.878,3.192);
\draw (A2) -- (B2) -- (C2) -- cycle;
\pic[draw,"$50^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.55cm,angle eccentricity=2.0] {angle=B2--A2--C2};
\pic[draw,"$70^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.48cm,angle eccentricity=2.1] {angle=C2--B2--A2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both triangles have sides of $7$ cm and $4$ cm and an angle of $25^{\circ}$, marked in matching positions.
What can be concluded?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.5,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.095,1.909);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$7\text{ cm}$};
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway,right,font=\small] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\pic[draw,"$25^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.8cm,angle eccentricity=1.7] {angle=B--A--C};
\coordinate (A2) at (5.6,0);
\coordinate (B2) at (9.1,0);
\coordinate (C2) at (7.255,0.772);
\draw (A2) -- (B2) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$7\text{ cm}$};
\draw (A2) -- (C2);
\draw (B2) -- (C2) node[midway,above right,font=\small] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\pic[draw,"$25^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.85cm,angle eccentricity=1.7] {angle=B2--A2--C2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In the diagram, $\angle D = \angle X = 52^{\circ}$ and $\angle E = \angle Y = 71^{\circ}$.
Which single extra piece of information proves $\Delta DEF \equiv \Delta XYZ$ by AAS?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.85]
\coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\coordinate (E) at (3.2,0);
\coordinate (F) at (2.221,2.843);
\draw (D) -- (E) -- (F) -- cycle;
\pic[draw,"$52^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.55cm,angle eccentricity=2.0] {angle=E--D--F};
\pic[draw,"$71^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.45cm,angle eccentricity=2.2] {angle=F--E--D};
\node[below left,font=\small] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below right,font=\small] at (E) {$E$};
\node[above,font=\small] at (F) {$F$};
\coordinate (X) at (7.6,0);
\coordinate (Y) at (4.4,0);
\coordinate (Z) at (5.379,2.843);
\draw (X) -- (Y) -- (Z) -- cycle;
\pic[draw,"$52^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.55cm,angle eccentricity=2.0] {angle=Z--X--Y};
\pic[draw,"$71^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.45cm,angle eccentricity=2.2] {angle=X--Y--Z};
\node[below right,font=\small] at (X) {$X$};
\node[below left,font=\small] at (Y) {$Y$};
\node[above,font=\small] at (Z) {$Z$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$ABCD$ is a parallelogram with diagonal $AC$ drawn, and its opposite sides are marked equal.
Using only the marked sides, which congruence test proves $\Delta ABC \equiv \Delta CDA$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=1.0]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tickmark2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.1,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.3,1.8);
\coordinate (D) at (1.2,1.8);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (B);
\draw[tickmark2] (B) -- (C);
\draw[tickmark] (C) -- (D);
\draw[tickmark2] (D) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\node[below left,font=\small] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right,font=\small] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right,font=\small] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left,font=\small] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

One triangle has angles of $55^{\circ}$ and $65^{\circ}$ marked and a $6$ cm side; the other has angles of $60^{\circ}$ and $55^{\circ}$ marked and a $6$ cm side, as shown.
What can be concluded?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.85]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.981,2.829);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway,above right,font=\small] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\pic[draw,"$55^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.55cm,angle eccentricity=2.0] {angle=B--A--C};
\pic[draw,"$65^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.48cm,angle eccentricity=2.1] {angle=C--B--A};
\coordinate (D) at (5.2,0);
\coordinate (E) at (8.321,0);
\coordinate (F) at (6.927,2.991);
\draw (D) -- (E) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\draw (D) -- (F);
\draw (E) -- (F);
\pic[draw,"$60^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.5cm,angle eccentricity=2.0] {angle=E--D--F};
\pic[draw,"$55^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.45cm,angle eccentricity=2.2] {angle=D--F--E};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Which congruence test proves that this pair of triangles is congruent?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.9]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (R) at (1.962,1.374);
\draw[tickmark] (P) -- (Q);
\draw (P) -- (R);
\draw (Q) -- (R);
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.6cm] {angle=Q--P--R};
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.4cm] {angle=R--Q--P};
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.52cm] {angle=R--Q--P};
\coordinate (P2) at (8.0,0);
\coordinate (Q2) at (4.4,0);
\coordinate (R2) at (6.038,1.374);
\draw[tickmark] (P2) -- (Q2);
\draw (P2) -- (R2);
\draw (Q2) -- (R2);
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.6cm] {angle=R2--P2--Q2};
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.4cm] {angle=P2--Q2--R2};
\pic[draw,angle radius=0.52cm] {angle=P2--Q2--R2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two of these three triangles are congruent.
Which pair?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.7]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.8,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0.829,1.369);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$7$};
\draw (A) -- (C) node[midway,above left,font=\small] {$4$};
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway,above right,font=\small] {$6$};
\node[below,font=\small] at (1.4,-0.55) {$\Delta 1$};
\coordinate (D) at (3.5,0);
\coordinate (E) at (6.3,0);
\coordinate (F) at (4.643,1.12);
\draw (D) -- (E) node[midway,below,font=\small] {$7$};
\draw (D) -- (F) node[midway,above left,font=\small] {$4$};
\draw (E) -- (F) node[midway,above right,font=\small] {$5$};
\node[below,font=\small] at (4.9,-0.55) {$\Delta 2$};
\coordinate (G) at (10.9,1.5);
\coordinate (H) at (8.1,1.5);
\coordinate (I) at (10.071,0.131);
\draw (G) -- (H) node[midway,above,font=\small] {$7$};
\draw (G) -- (I) node[midway,below right,font=\small] {$4$};
\draw (H) -- (I) node[midway,below left,font=\small] {$6$};
\node[below,font=\small] at (9.5,-0.55) {$\Delta 3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
